window.previewChapters = [{"number": 1, "title": "Adding large random matrices", "subtitle": "Why eigenvalues alone do not tell us the spectrum of a sum", "description": "Eigenvector geometry, normalized traces, and a first calculation with free independence.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "eigenvalues-are-not-enough", "title": "Eigenvalues are not enough"}, {"id": "the-trace-turns-a-matrix-into-a-distribution", "title": "The trace turns a matrix into a distribution"}, {"id": "a-small-amount-of-noncommutative-probability", "title": "Noncommutative probability"}, {"id": "freeness-is-a-rule-for-centered-alternating-products", "title": "Freeness is a rule for centered, alternating products"}, {"id": "the-first-calculation-that-distinguishes-freeness", "title": "A fourth-moment calculation"}, {"id": "what-does-that-predict-for-a-sum", "title": "Adding two free sign variables"}, {"id": "when-does-the-matrix-model-justify-this-rule", "title": "Asymptotic freeness of random matrices"}, {"id": "try-it-before-continuing", "title": "Exercises"}, {"id": "further-reading", "title": "Further reading"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_n\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_n\\)</span> are two independent <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\times n\\)</span> Hermitian random matrices. We understand the eigenvalues of each matrix separately. What should we expect from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_n+B_n\\)</span>?</p>\n<p>For ordinary independent random variables, the two distributions determine the distribution of the sum. Matrices make the question more interesting: their eigenvectors have a relative geometry, and that geometry affects addition.</p>\n<p>Free probability gives us a way to calculate when those eigenvectors are sufficiently mixed. Independence of the random matrices, on its own, will not be enough.</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"eigenvalues-are-not-enough\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"eigenvalues-are-not-enough\">Eigenvalues are not enough</h2>\n<p>Take <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nA=B_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad B_2=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</span> Both choices of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span> have spectrum <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{-1,1\\}\\)</span>. Nevertheless, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{spec}(A+B_1)=\\{-2,2\\},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{spec}(A+B_2)=\\{0,0\\}.\n\\]</span> The individual spectra have lost the information about which directions line up.</p>\n<p>Writing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A=U\\Lambda U^*\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=V\\Gamma V^*\\)</span> makes the missing ingredient explicit: the spectrum of their sum depends on the relative basis <span class=\"math inline\">\\(U^*V\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Here is a useful replacement for choosing an alignment. Keep deterministic diagonal matrices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_n,E_n\\)</span>, draw a <strong>Haar unitary</strong> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(U_n\\)</span>, and study <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nD_n+U_nE_nU_n^*.\n\\]</span> Haar measure is the uniform probability measure on the unitary group, in the precise sense of invariance under multiplication by any fixed unitary. Conjugation preserves the spectrum of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_n\\)</span> and randomizes its eigenbasis.</p>\n<p>Under suitable hypotheses, this produces a limiting rule for mixed moments. That rule is freeness.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"the-trace-turns-a-matrix-into-a-distribution\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"the-trace-turns-a-matrix-into-a-distribution\">The trace turns a matrix into a distribution</h2>\n<p>Define the empirical spectral measure and normalized trace by <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mu_A=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\delta_{\\lambda_j(A)},\n\\qquad \\tau_n(A)=\\frac1n\\operatorname{Tr}(A).\n\\]</span> Then <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\tau_n(A^k)=\\int x^k\\,d\\mu_A(x).}\n\\]</span> A normalized trace of a power is exactly a spectral moment. The normalization gives total mass one, so moments remain comparable as the dimension changes.</p>\n<p>To study the sum, expand <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau_n((A+B)^k)\\)</span>. Now words such as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(AABB\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(ABAB\\)</span> appear. They cannot generally be rearranged into each other. Trace is cyclic, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau(ABBA)=\\tau(AABB)\\)</span>, but it does not permit arbitrary permutations.</p>\n<p>For a random matrix, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau_n(A^k)\\)</span> is itself random. Its ensemble average is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[\\tau_n(A^k)]\\)</span>. We will distinguish these when discussing experiments and convergence.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"a-small-amount-of-noncommutative-probability\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"a-small-amount-of-noncommutative-probability\">Noncommutative probability</h2>\n<p>Our setting is a unital algebra <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)</span> with an adjoint operation and a normalized positive trace <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau\\)</span>. Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau\\)</span> is linear and satisfies <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(1)=1,\\qquad \\tau(x^*x)\\geq0,\\qquad \\tau(xy)=\\tau(yx).\n\\]</span> Matrices with normalized trace are the finite-dimensional example. For bounded self-adjoint operators, the spectral theorem gives a probability law whose moments are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau(a^k)\\)</span>. We use compactly supported laws throughout the series, so their moments determine them uniquely.</p>\n<p>Think of a self-adjoint element <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=a^*\\)</span> as a real random variable and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau\\)</span> as expectation. Multiplication may fail to commute, but positivity and spectral measures remain available.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"freeness-is-a-rule-for-centered-alternating-products\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"freeness-is-a-rule-for-centered-alternating-products\">Freeness is a rule for centered, alternating products</h2>\n<p>Unital subalgebras <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal A_1,\\mathcal A_2,\\ldots\\)</span> are <strong>free</strong> if <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(x_1x_2\\cdots x_m)=0\n\\]</span> whenever each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span> belongs to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal A_{j_i}\\)</span>, consecutive indices differ, and every factor is centered: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nj_i\\neq j_{i+1},\\qquad \\tau(x_i)=0.\n\\]</span> We call <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span> free when the unital algebras they generate are free. The condition therefore applies to centered polynomials in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>, not merely to the original variables.</p>\n<p>Why center? Because every factor can be split into its mean and its fluctuation. Expanding these splits reduces mixed moments to simpler ones until the alternating-centered rule applies. This recursively determines the joint moments from the two marginal moment sequences.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"the-first-calculation-that-distinguishes-freeness\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"the-first-calculation-that-distinguishes-freeness\">A fourth-moment calculation</h2>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a,b\\)</span> are centered and free, with <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(a^2)=\\alpha,\\qquad \\tau(b^2)=\\beta.\n\\]</span> The definition immediately gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(ab)=0,\\qquad \\tau(abab)=0.\n\\]</span> But <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a^2b^2\\)</span> is different. Center the squares: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n0=\\tau\\bigl((a^2-\\alpha1)(b^2-\\beta1)\\bigr)\n  =\\tau(a^2b^2)-\\alpha\\beta.\n\\]</span> Consequently, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\tau(a^2b^2)=\\alpha\\beta,\\qquad \\tau(abab)=0.}\n\\]</span> For centered, classically independent scalar variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X,Y\\)</span> with those variances, both corresponding expressions equal <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha\\beta\\)</span>, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(XYXY=X^2Y^2\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>At second order, both theories give variance addition. At fourth order, the distinction becomes visible: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau((a+b)^4)=\\tau(a^4)+\\tau(b^4)+4\\alpha\\beta.\n\\]</span> To see the coefficient four, list the six words containing two of each letter. The words <span class=\"math inline\">\\(aabb,abba,baab,bbaa\\)</span> each contribute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha\\beta\\)</span>. The alternating words <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab,baba\\)</span> vanish. Words with three of one letter and one of the other also vanish, by centering and cyclicity.</p>\n<p>Classical independence instead gives a coefficient of six.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"what-does-that-predict-for-a-sum\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"what-does-that-predict-for-a-sum\">Adding two free sign variables</h2>\n<p>Let both marginal laws be <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mu=\\tfrac12\\delta_{-1}+\\tfrac12\\delta_1.\n\\]</span> Each variable is centered, with second and fourth moments equal to one. The free sum therefore has <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nm_2=2,\\qquad m_4=6.\n\\]</span> The classical sum has masses <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/4,1/2,1/4\\)</span> at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(-2,0,2\\)</span>, and fourth moment eight. Perfect alignment, where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b=a\\)</span>, gives fourth moment sixteen.</p>\n<p>We write the law of a free sum as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu\\boxplus\\nu\\)</span>, the <strong>additive free convolution</strong>. Part III will calculate the entire law in this example. Part IV will compare all three eigenbasis choices numerically.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"when-does-the-matrix-model-justify-this-rule\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"when-does-the-matrix-model-justify-this-rule\">Asymptotic freeness of random matrices</h2>\n<p>One sufficient setting is that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_n,E_n\\)</span> are deterministic Hermitian matrices with uniformly bounded operator norms and convergent empirical spectral measures. If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(U_n\\)</span> is Haar unitary, then the pair <span class=\"math inline\">\\((D_n,U_nE_nU_n^*)\\)</span> converges in joint normalized-trace moments to a free pair, in probability. This is a standard asymptotic-freeness theorem, not something implied just by our definition. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1908.08125v2#S7\">Speicher, Section 7</a>.</p>\n<p>At each finite dimension, the pair generally fails exact freeness. The theorem describes the limit for each fixed word. Independent random diagonal sign matrices illustrate why ordinary independence alone is insufficient: they commute, and their alternating fourth moment is identically one.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau((a+b)^2)\\)</span> for centered free variables of variances <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha,\\beta\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>For the symmetric two-point laws above, compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau((a+b)^3)\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Explain why the identity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a^2=b^2=1\\)</span> is compatible with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau(abab)=0\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>The cross terms vanish, leaving <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha+\\beta\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>It is zero. The pure cubic moments vanish by symmetry, and the mixed terms vanish by freeness and cyclicity.</li>\n<li>The squares only simplify adjacent equal factors. Simplifying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a^2b^2\\)</span> would require commutativity.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"further-reading\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"further-reading\">Further reading</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/02/10/245a-notes-5-free-probability/\">Terence Tao’s Notes 5</a> develop the probability-space viewpoint and mixed-moment examples. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.08125\">Speicher’s lecture notes</a> give the definitions in Section 1 and the random-matrix connection in Section 7. The worked calculations here use the conventions stated above.</p>\n<p><a data-chapter=\"2\" href=\"?page=article&amp;chapter=2\">Next: noncrossing partitions and cumulants</a></p>\n</section>\n"}, {"number": 2, "title": "Why crossings disappear", "subtitle": "Noncrossing partitions, free cumulants, and the semicircle law", "description": "Organizing mixed moments with partitions, then deriving the free central limit theorem in moments.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "draw-the-positions-before-manipulating-the-variables", "title": "Crossing and noncrossing partitions"}, {"id": "cumulants-are-defined-recursively-from-moments", "title": "Cumulants are defined recursively from moments"}, {"id": "the-theorem-that-makes-the-bookkeeping-useful", "title": "Mixed free cumulants vanish"}, {"id": "a-distribution-made-entirely-of-pairs", "title": "Semicircle moments count noncrossing pairings"}, {"id": "why-a-free-central-limit-theorem-follows", "title": "Why a free central limit theorem follows"}, {"id": "try-it-before-continuing", "title": "Exercises"}, {"id": "further-reading", "title": "Further reading"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>In <a data-chapter=\"1\" href=\"?page=article&amp;chapter=1\">Part I</a>, the words <span class=\"math inline\">\\(aabb\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab\\)</span> behaved differently. For centered free variables, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(aabb)=\\tau(a^2)\\tau(b^2),\\qquad \\tau(abab)=0.\n\\]</span> Expanding every power by hand will become painful quickly. We need a way to organize the words. The useful bookkeeping objects are partitions whose blocks do not cross.</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"draw-the-positions-before-manipulating-the-variables\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"draw-the-positions-before-manipulating-the-variables\">Crossing and noncrossing partitions</h2>\n<p>A partition divides <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\)</span> into disjoint nonempty blocks. It is <strong>noncrossing</strong> if there are no indices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i&lt;j&lt;k&lt;\\ell\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,k\\)</span> in one block and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(j,\\ell\\)</span> in a different block.</p>\n<p>For four positions, the three pairings are <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\{\\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\}\\},\\quad\n\\{\\{1,4\\},\\{2,3\\}\\},\\quad\n\\{\\{1,3\\},\\{2,4\\}\\}.\n\\]</span> Only the last one crosses when the positions are placed around a circle.</p>\n<div class=\"quarto-figure quarto-figure-center\">\n<figure class=\"figure\">\n<p><a aria-label=\"Open figure at full size in a new tab\" class=\"figure-link\" data-gallery=\"quarto-lightbox-gallery-1\" href=\"assets/pairings.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Two noncrossing pairings and the crossing pairing on four cyclically ordered positions.\"><img alt=\"Four numbered corners in each panel. Adjacent and nested pairings do not cross. Pairing 1 with 3 and 2 with 4 makes intersecting diagonals.\" class=\"img-fluid figure-img\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/pairings.svg\"/></a></p>\n\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab\\)</span>, pairing equal letters requires precisely the crossing pairing. For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(aabb\\)</span>, it requires the first noncrossing pairing. This is the diagrammatic trace of our earlier calculation. To make it a general method, we introduce cumulants.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"cumulants-are-defined-recursively-from-moments\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"cumulants-are-defined-recursively-from-moments\">Cumulants are defined recursively from moments</h2>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(NC(n)\\)</span> for the set of noncrossing partitions of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> positions. The <strong>free cumulants</strong> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_n\\)</span> are the multilinear functionals defined by <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(x_1\\cdots x_n)\n=\\sum_{\\pi\\in NC(n)}\\kappa_\\pi(x_1,\\ldots,x_n),\n\\]</span> where <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\kappa_\\pi(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\n=\\prod_{V\\in\\pi}\\kappa_{|V|}(x_{i_1},\\ldots,x_{i_{|V|}}),\n\\qquad V=\\{i_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;i_{|V|}\\}.\n\\]</span> The factors on the right are scalars, so their product commutes. Within each cumulant, the original order of the arguments is retained.</p>\n<p>This is a recursive definition: the one-block partition contributes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_n\\)</span>, and all other blocks have smaller size. Once cumulants of lower orders are known, solve for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_n\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>For one variable, abbreviate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_n=\\tau(a^n)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_n=\\kappa_n(a,\\ldots,a)\\)</span>. The first relations are <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_1&amp;=\\kappa_1,\\\\\nm_2&amp;=\\kappa_2+\\kappa_1^2,\\\\\nm_3&amp;=\\kappa_3+3\\kappa_2\\kappa_1+\\kappa_1^3,\\\\\nm_4&amp;=\\kappa_4+4\\kappa_3\\kappa_1+2\\kappa_2^2\n       +6\\kappa_2\\kappa_1^2+\\kappa_1^4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span> That coefficient two counts the noncrossing pairings on four positions. Classical cumulants use all set partitions, so the corresponding coefficient is three.</p>\n<p>For a centered variable, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\kappa_2=m_2,\\qquad \\kappa_3=m_3,\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\kappa_4=m_4-2m_2^2.}\n\\]</span> For a symmetric sign variable, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_2=m_4=1\\)</span>, giving <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_4=-1\\)</span>. A negative fourth free cumulant is entirely possible.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"the-theorem-that-makes-the-bookkeeping-useful\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"the-theorem-that-makes-the-bookkeeping-useful\">Mixed free cumulants vanish</h2>\n<p>The crucial result is the <strong>mixed-cumulant characterization of freeness</strong>: unital subalgebras are free exactly when every mixed free cumulant vanishes. Here mixed means that its arguments come from more than one of the subalgebras. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1908.08125v2#S3\">Speicher, Section 3</a>.</p>\n<p>This theorem connects the combinatorial definition to the alternating-centered definition. We use it here without reproducing its full induction proof.</p>\n<p>For centered free <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a,b\\)</span>, apply the moment formula to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab\\)</span>. Singleton blocks vanish by centering. Blocks containing both letters vanish by the theorem. Pairing equal letters would cross. Every contribution is therefore zero.</p>\n<p>Now expand a cumulant of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a+b\\)</span> by multilinearity. Every mixed choice of arguments vanishes, leaving only the all-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> and all-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span> terms: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\kappa_n(a+b)=\\kappa_n(a)+\\kappa_n(b).}\n\\]</span> The full moment sequence does not add. The cumulant sequence does.</p>\n<p>For the sum of two free symmetric sign variables, the second cumulant is two and the fourth is minus two. Hence <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nm_4=-2+2(2)^2=6,\n\\]</span> recovering Part I without listing sixteen words.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"a-distribution-made-entirely-of-pairs\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"a-distribution-made-entirely-of-pairs\">Semicircle moments count noncrossing pairings</h2>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> has cumulants <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\kappa_1(s)=0,\\quad \\kappa_2(s)=v,\\quad\n\\kappa_n(s)=0\\quad(n\\geq3).\n\\]</span> Only partitions into pairs survive. Odd moments vanish, and <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(s^{2k})=C_kv^k,\n\\qquad C_k=\\frac1{k+1}\\binom{2k}{k}.\n\\]</span> Why Catalan numbers? Pair the first position with position <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2j+2\\)</span>. Noncrossing forces the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2j\\)</span> positions inside that pair and the remaining positions outside it to pair separately. If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_0=1\\)</span>, this gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nC_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}C_jC_{k-1-j}.\n\\]</span> Thus the even moments begin <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1,v,2v^2,5v^3,14v^4\\)</span> when the initial one is the zeroth moment.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v&gt;0\\)</span>, these are the moments of the <strong>semicircle density</strong> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\rho_v(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4v-x^2}}{2\\pi v}\n\\,\\mathbf1_{\\{|x|\\leq2\\sqrt v\\}}.\n\\]</span> The substitution <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x=2\\sqrt v\\cos\\theta\\)</span> verifies the moments by a beta integral. In particular, its variance is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>, and its fourth moment is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2v^2\\)</span>. When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v=0\\)</span>, the law is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta_0\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"why-a-free-central-limit-theorem-follows\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"why-a-free-central-limit-theorem-follows\">Why a free central limit theorem follows</h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a_1,a_2,\\ldots\\)</span> be freely independent, identically distributed, bounded self-adjoint variables, centered with variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>. Set <span class=\"math display\">\\[\ns_N=\\frac{a_1+\\cdots+a_N}{\\sqrt N}.\n\\]</span> Multilinearity gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_k(ca)=c^k\\kappa_k(a)\\)</span>. Additivity then yields <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\kappa_k(s_N)=N^{1-k/2}\\kappa_k(a_1).\n\\]</span> The first cumulant is zero, the second remains <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>, and every fixed higher cumulant tends to zero. Since each fixed moment is a finite polynomial in cumulants, the moments converge to those of the semicircle law.</p>\n<p>This also gives weak convergence of the laws. The common second moment ensures tightness. Convergence of higher even moments controls the tails needed to pass moments to any weak subsequential limit. The compactly supported semicircle law is moment-determinate, so every such limit is the same.</p>\n<p>The normal and semicircle laws play parallel roles: classical independent sums retain all pairings in their limiting moments, while free sums retain the noncrossing pairings.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Find the fourth cumulant of the variance-one semicircle law.</li>\n<li>Find the sixth moment of a centered semicircular variable with variance two.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span> is another free copy of a centered variance-one <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>, express <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau((a+b)^4)\\)</span> in terms of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_4(a)\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(2-2(1)^2=0\\)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_3\\,2^3=5\\cdot8=40\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>It is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2m_4(a)+4\\)</span>. Equivalently, add the fourth cumulants and use the new variance two.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"further-reading\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"further-reading\">Further reading</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/s/s39speicher.pdf\">Speicher’s survey on noncrossing partitions</a> explains the combinatorial foundations. <a href=\"https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/speicher/publikationen/Nica-Speicher.pdf\">Nica and Speicher’s book</a>, Lectures 8 through 12, develops the free central limit theorem and cumulant machinery in detail.</p>\n\n</section>\n"}, {"number": 3, "title": "Calculating the law of a sum", "subtitle": "The R-transform turns free addition into ordinary addition", "description": "Derive the Cauchy-transform equation and calculate why two free sign variables have an arcsine sum.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "a-transform-that-remembers-the-law", "title": "The Cauchy transform"}, {"id": "from-the-moment-formula-to-a-functional-equation", "title": "From the moment formula to a functional equation"}, {"id": "warm-up-adding-semicircles", "title": "Warm-up: adding semicircles"}, {"id": "the-main-example-two-sign-variables", "title": "Adding two free sign variables"}, {"id": "check-the-result-without-transforms", "title": "Checking the moments"}, {"id": "try-it-before-continuing", "title": "Exercises"}, {"id": "further-reading", "title": "Further reading"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>We know that the free sum of two symmetric sign variables has second moment two and fourth moment six. What is its entire distribution?</p>\n<p><a data-chapter=\"2\" href=\"?page=article&amp;chapter=2\">Part II</a> supplied an additive sequence of numbers: the free cumulants. Packaging those numbers into a function lets us solve this question with a quadratic equation and a careful choice of square root.</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"a-transform-that-remembers-the-law\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"a-transform-that-remembers-the-law\">The Cauchy transform</h2>\n<p>For a compactly supported probability measure <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu\\)</span> on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)</span>, define its <strong>Cauchy transform</strong> by <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG_\\mu(z)=\\int\\frac{1}{z-x}\\,d\\mu(x),\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z&gt;0.\n\\]</span> Our sign convention matters. With <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/(z-x)\\)</span>, the imaginary part of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is negative in the upper half-plane. Some references use the negative of this transform.</p>\n<p>For sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(|z|\\)</span>, expand the geometric series: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG_\\mu(z)=\\frac1z+\\frac{m_1}{z^2}+\\frac{m_2}{z^3}+\\cdots.\n\\]</span> Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> encodes every moment, and its leading behavior must be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/z\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>It also recovers the density. Indeed, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n-\\frac1\\pi\\operatorname{Im}G_\\mu(x+i\\eta)\n=\\int\\frac{\\eta}{\\pi((x-t)^2+\\eta^2)}\\,d\\mu(t).\n\\]</span> The right side smooths the measure with a Cauchy kernel. At continuity points of a density <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho\\)</span>, letting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho(x)\\)</span>. More generally, Stieltjes inversion recovers masses of intervals. An atom at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_0\\)</span> can be checked separately through <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mu(\\{x_0\\})=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}i\\eta G_\\mu(x_0+i\\eta).\n\\]</span></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"from-the-moment-formula-to-a-functional-equation\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"from-the-moment-formula-to-a-functional-equation\">From the moment formula to a functional equation</h2>\n<p>Introduce the two generating series <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nM(t)=1+\\sum_{n\\geq1}m_nt^n,\n\\qquad R(w)=\\sum_{n\\geq1}\\kappa_nw^{n-1}.\n\\]</span> Look at the block containing position 1 in a noncrossing partition. If that block has size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>, it leaves <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> ordered gaps, each carrying an independent noncrossing partition. This decomposes the moment formula into <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nM(t)=1+\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\kappa_k t^kM(t)^k\n=1+tM(t)R(tM(t)).\n\\]</span> Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G(z)=z^{-1}M(z^{-1})\\)</span>, substitution gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nzG(z)=1+G(z)R(G(z)),\n\\]</span> or <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{z=\\frac1{G(z)}+R(G(z)).}\n\\]</span> Initially this identity holds near infinity, or as a formal Laurent-series identity. We should not assume that the power series for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R\\)</span> converges at every value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Equivalently, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\)</span> denote the local compositional inverse of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> near infinity, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K(w)\\sim1/w\\)</span> near zero. Then <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nR(w)=K(w)-\\frac1w.\n\\]</span> This is the <strong>R-transform</strong>. Cumulant additivity becomes <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{R_{\\mu\\boxplus\\nu}(w)=R_\\mu(w)+R_\\nu(w).}\n\\]</span> The local inverse and analytic extension are developed rigorously in <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1908.08125v2#S4\">Speicher, Theorem 4.12</a>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"warm-up-adding-semicircles\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"warm-up-adding-semicircles\">Warm-up: adding semicircles</h2>\n<p>A centered semicircular variable of variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span> has only one nonzero cumulant, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_2=v\\)</span>. Therefore <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R(w)=vw\\)</span> and <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nvG(z)^2-zG(z)+1=0.\n\\]</span> For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v&gt;0\\)</span>, choose the solution <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG(z)=\\frac{z-\\sqrt{z^2-4v}}{2v},\n\\]</span> where the square root is the branch asymptotic to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span> at infinity. The other solution grows like <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z/v\\)</span>, which cannot be the transform of a probability measure.</p>\n<p>Taking the boundary value in the upper half-plane recovers the semicircle density. Adding free semicircles of variances <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_1,v_2\\)</span> simply replaces <span class=\"math inline\">\\(vw\\)</span> by <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v_1+v_2)w\\)</span>. Their sum is semicircular with variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_1+v_2\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"the-main-example-two-sign-variables\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"the-main-example-two-sign-variables\">Adding two free sign variables</h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu=\\tfrac12\\delta_{-1}+\\tfrac12\\delta_1\\)</span>. Its transform is immediate: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG_\\mu(z)=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{z-1}+\\frac1{z+1}\\right)\n=\\frac{z}{z^2-1}.\n\\]</span> Solve <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=z/(z^2-1)\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span>: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nz=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt{1+4w^2}}{2w}.\n\\]</span> The inverse with a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/w\\)</span> pole uses the plus sign, with the square root analytic and equal to one at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=0\\)</span>. Hence <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nR_\\mu(w)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+4w^2}-1}{2w}.\n\\]</span> Expanding gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R_\\mu(w)=w-w^3+2w^5-\\cdots\\)</span>, consistent with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_2=1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\kappa_4=-1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\nu=\\mu\\boxplus\\mu\\)</span>, add the two R-transforms. The equation for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_\\nu\\)</span> becomes <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nz=\\frac1{G_\\nu(z)}+\n\\frac{\\sqrt{1+4G_\\nu(z)^2}-1}{G_\\nu(z)}\n=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+4G_\\nu(z)^2}}{G_\\nu(z)}.\n\\]</span> Squaring yields <span class=\"math inline\">\\((z^2-4)G_\\nu(z)^2=1\\)</span>. Choose the branch matching <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_\\nu(z)\\sim1/z\\)</span>: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{G_\\nu(z)=\\frac1{\\sqrt{z^2-4}}.}\n\\]</span> Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{z^2-4}\\)</span> means the analytic branch on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb C\\setminus[-2,2]\\)</span> asymptotic to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span>. It is not the pointwise principal square root of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z^2-4\\)</span> everywhere. The formula agrees with the transform near infinity and continues analytically to the upper half-plane.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(-2&lt;x&lt;2\\)</span>, its boundary value is <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG_\\nu(x+i0)=-\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}.\n\\]</span> Therefore the answer is the <strong>arcsine law</strong>: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{d\\nu(x)=\\frac{\\mathbf1_{(-2,2)}(x)}{\\pi\\sqrt{4-x^2}}\\,dx.}\n\\]</span> The density diverges at the endpoints, but its integral is one. These integrable divergences are not atoms: multiplying the transform by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\eta\\)</span> at either endpoint gives zero in the limit.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"check-the-result-without-transforms\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"check-the-result-without-transforms\">Checking the moments</h2>\n<p>Set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x=2\\cos\\theta\\)</span>. The arcsine measure becomes uniform <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\theta/\\pi\\)</span> on <span class=\"math inline\">\\([0,\\pi]\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\int x^2\\,d\\nu(x)=2,\\qquad\n\\int x^4\\,d\\nu(x)=6.\n\\]</span> More generally, its odd moments vanish and its <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2k\\)</span>th moment is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\binom{2k}{k}\\)</span>. This agrees with our mixed-word calculations.</p>\n<p>Two free sign variables produce an arcsine law. Many free sign variables, summed and divided by the square root of their number, approach a semicircle. These are different questions, so there is no conflict between the two answers.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Find <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R_{\\delta_c}\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Derive the transform rule for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(ca\\)</span> from cumulant scaling.</li>\n<li>Why is solving a polynomial equation for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> insufficient without a branch condition?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G(z)=1/(z-c)\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K(w)=c+1/w\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R(w)=c\\)</span>. Free addition of a constant translates the law.</li>\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(R_{ca}(w)=cR_a(cw)\\)</span>, since the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>th cumulant scales by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c^n\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>A polynomial may have several solutions. A probability Cauchy transform must be analytic in the upper half-plane, have negative imaginary part there, and behave like <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/z\\)</span> at infinity. Squaring can introduce extraneous branches.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"further-reading\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"further-reading\">Further reading</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1908.08125v2#S4\">Speicher’s lecture notes, Section 4</a> contain the analytic R-transform theory and the symmetric two-point example. <a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/02/10/245a-notes-5-free-probability/\">Tao’s free probability notes</a> give another route from transforms to free convolution. Check the sign convention when moving between references.</p>\n\n</section>\n"}, {"number": 4, "title": "Rotate the eigenvectors", "subtitle": "One spectrum, three alignments, three different sums", "description": "A reproducible Haar-unitary experiment tests the arcsine prediction and distinguishes independence from freeness.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "three-ways-to-use-exactly-the-same-eigenvalues", "title": "Three ways to use exactly the same eigenvalues"}, {"id": "sampling-a-haar-unitary-correctly", "title": "Sampling a Haar unitary correctly"}, {"id": "test-a-mixed-moment-not-just-a-histogram", "title": "Test a mixed moment, not just a histogram"}, {"id": "what-the-limiting-statement-actually-says", "title": "Large matrices and finite-size errors"}, {"id": "try-it-before-continuing", "title": "Exercises"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>The calculation in <a data-chapter=\"3\" href=\"?page=article&amp;chapter=3\">Part III</a> predicts an arcsine law when we add two free variables whose laws each put half their mass at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(-1\\)</span> and half at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Now we can test that prediction. Keep the eigenvalues fixed and change only the relative eigenvectors. This makes the missing ingredient from Part I visible.</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"three-ways-to-use-exactly-the-same-eigenvalues\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"three-ways-to-use-exactly-the-same-eigenvalues\">Three ways to use exactly the same eigenvalues</h2>\n<p>For even <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nD_n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\underbrace{1,\\ldots,1}_{n/2},\n\\underbrace{-1,\\ldots,-1}_{n/2}).\n\\]</span> Compare these choices:</p>\n<table class=\"caption-top table\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\"/>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\"/>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\"/>\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Second matrix</th>\n<th>Relative eigenbasis</th>\n<th>Prediction for the sum</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_n\\)</span></td>\n<td>Perfectly aligned</td>\n<td>Half the eigenvalues at each of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(-2,2\\)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><span class=\"math inline\">\\(P_nD_nP_n^*\\)</span></td>\n<td>A uniform random permutation</td>\n<td>Limiting masses <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/4,1/2,1/4\\)</span> at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(-2,0,2\\)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><span class=\"math inline\">\\(U_nD_nU_n^*\\)</span></td>\n<td>A Haar-random unitary rotation</td>\n<td>Limiting arcsine density on <span class=\"math inline\">\\([-2,2]\\)</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A permutation randomizes which signs match, but both matrices remain diagonal. Its empirical matching frequencies approach the product of the two sign distributions. A Haar unitary also mixes the coordinate directions themselves.</p>\n<div class=\"quarto-figure quarto-figure-center\">\n<figure class=\"figure\">\n<p><a aria-label=\"Open figure at full size in a new tab\" class=\"figure-link\" data-gallery=\"quarto-lightbox-gallery-1\" href=\"assets/sum-comparison.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Identical marginal spectra produce a two-point sum, a three-point sum, or an approximately arcsine sum, depending on the relative eigenbasis.\"><img alt=\"Three panels. Aligned matrices give masses one half at minus two and two. Randomly permuted signs give approximately one quarter, one half, one quarter at minus two, zero, two. Haar rotations give a continuous U-shaped histogram matching the arcsine curve.\" class=\"img-fluid figure-img\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/sum-comparison.svg\"/></a></p>\n\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p>The Haar panel pools six independent draws at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n=384\\)</span>. The other two panels each show one matrix pair at that dimension. The vertical axes distinguish discrete probability mass from continuous density. The density’s endpoint singularity also makes the outer histogram bars sensitive to bin width.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"sampling-a-haar-unitary-correctly\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"sampling-a-haar-unitary-correctly\">Sampling a Haar unitary correctly</h2>\n<p>Start with a complex Gaussian matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z\\)</span> and compute a QR decomposition. Correct the phases on the diagonal of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(R\\)</span> so that the resulting triangular factor has positive real diagonal. The corresponding unitary factor has Haar distribution. This is the QR construction described by <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0609050\">Mezzadri</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-copy-outer-scaffold\"><div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode python code-with-copy\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-1\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"im\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as</span> np</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-2\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-3\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>rng <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">1729</span>)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-4\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-5\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"kw\">def</span> haar_unitary(n):</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-6\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>    z <span class=\"op\">=</span> (rng.normal(size<span class=\"op\">=</span>(n, n))</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-7\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>         <span class=\"op\">+</span> <span class=\"ot\">1j</span> <span class=\"op\">*</span> rng.normal(size<span class=\"op\">=</span>(n, n))) <span class=\"op\">/</span> np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2</span>)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-8\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>    q, r <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.linalg.qr(z)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-9\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>    d <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.diag(r)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-10\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>    <span class=\"cf\">return</span> q <span class=\"op\">*</span> (d <span class=\"op\">/</span> np.<span class=\"bu\">abs</span>(d))[<span class=\"va\">None</span>, :]</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-11\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-12\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>n <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"dv\">384</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-13\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>signs <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.r_[np.ones(n <span class=\"op\">//</span> <span class=\"dv\">2</span>), <span class=\"op\">-</span>np.ones(n <span class=\"op\">//</span> <span class=\"dv\">2</span>)]</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-14\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>a <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.diag(signs)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-15\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>u <span class=\"op\">=</span> haar_unitary(n)</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-16\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>b <span class=\"op\">=</span> (u <span class=\"op\">*</span> signs) <span class=\"op\">@</span> u.conj().T</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a aria-hidden=\"true\" href=\"#cb1-17\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>spectrum <span class=\"op\">=</span> np.linalg.eigvalsh(a <span class=\"op\">+</span> b)</span></code></pre></div></div>\n<p>The zero-diagonal event has probability zero for an exact Gaussian draw. Column multiplication by <code>signs</code> implements <span class=\"math inline\">\\(UD_n\\)</span>, and the conjugate transpose is essential for complex matrices.</p>\n<p>The phase correction makes this a general Haar sampler. In this particular conjugation experiment, those column phases cancel against the diagonal <span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_n\\)</span>, but that cancellation would not justify calling an arbitrary uncorrected QR output Haar.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"test-a-mixed-moment-not-just-a-histogram\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"test-a-mixed-moment-not-just-a-histogram\">Test a mixed moment, not just a histogram</h2>\n<p>The one-variable spectrum of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A+B\\)</span> checks one consequence of freeness. We can also check a word that appears directly in the definition: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nT_n=\\frac1n\\operatorname{Tr}(ABAB).\n\\]</span> Both <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A=D_n\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=U_nD_nU_n^*\\)</span> are exactly centered. Freeness predicts <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T_n\\to0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>For the aligned and permuted cases, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span> commute and square to the identity, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(ABAB=I\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T_n=1\\)</span> exactly. This is a particularly clear distinction.</p>\n<p>For ten independent Haar draws at each size, the recorded results are:</p>\n<div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<table class=\"caption-top table\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\"/>\n<col style=\"width: 26%\"/>\n<col style=\"width: 26%\"/>\n<col style=\"width: 26%\"/>\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Mean second moment</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Mean fourth moment</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">RMS alternating moment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>64</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2.00503</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6.00984</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.02210</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>128</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2.00004</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5.99541</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.01335</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>256</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2.00121</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6.01167</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00522</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>The limiting second and fourth moments are 2 and 6. The last column is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{\\frac1{10}\\sum_jT_{n,j}^2}\\)</span>, rather than the signed average, so positive and negative fluctuations cannot cancel.</p>\n<div class=\"quarto-figure quarto-figure-center\">\n<figure class=\"figure\">\n<p><a aria-label=\"Open figure at full size in a new tab\" class=\"figure-link\" data-gallery=\"quarto-lightbox-gallery-2\" href=\"assets/mixed-moment.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"The observed root mean square alternating moment becomes smaller across the three tested matrix dimensions.\"><img alt=\"A line plot of the root mean square normalized trace of ABAB at dimensions 64, 128, and 256, decreasing from about 0.022 to 0.013 to 0.005.\" class=\"img-fluid figure-img\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/mixed-moment.svg\"/></a></p>\n\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p>These observations agree with the theorem. Three dimensions and ten draws do not establish a convergence rate, and the displayed trend need not be monotone for a different random seed.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"what-the-limiting-statement-actually-says\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"what-the-limiting-statement-actually-says\">Large matrices and finite-size errors</h2>\n<p>For deterministic bounded Hermitian sequences with convergent marginal laws, the Haar-rotation theorem controls joint normalized-trace moments for each fixed noncommutative polynomial. The uniformly bounded spectra in this example then let us turn moment convergence for the sum into weak convergence of its empirical spectral measure. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1908.08125v2#S7\">Speicher, Section 7</a>.</p>\n<p>This does not say that a finite pair is exactly free. It also does not, by itself, provide a finite-size error bound, eigenvalue-spacing statistics, or convergence of the largest eigenvalue.</p>\n<p>A simple example explains the last distinction. The matrices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{diag}(10,0,\\ldots,0)\\)</span> have all fixed positive normalized-trace moments tending to zero, but their largest eigenvalue remains ten. A vanishing fraction of outliers can disappear from a limiting spectral measure. Stronger random-matrix results address such questions, but they require stronger conclusions than ordinary moment convergence.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Replace the Haar unitary by the identity. What happens to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T_n\\)</span>?</li>\n<li>Why would subtracting only the ensemble mean be insufficient for a general finite-sample centered-moment diagnostic?</li>\n<li>Would a good histogram of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A+B\\)</span> prove that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span> are asymptotically free?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(A=B=D_n\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(ABAB=I\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T_n=1\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>A particular matrix may still have nonzero normalized trace. If the diagnostic requires centered factors, use <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A-\\tau_n(A)I\\)</span> and similarly for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span>, or account for those fluctuations. Our balanced signs are exactly centered already.</li>\n<li>No. Freeness constrains all alternating products of centered polynomials. One spectral histogram tests only a small part of that joint structure.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n\n</section>\n"}, {"number": 5, "title": "Multiplying positive matrices", "subtitle": "The S-transform and the geometry hidden in a product", "description": "Define multiplicative free convolution, derive the S-transform convention, and work through free projections.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "put-the-product-in-a-positive-form", "title": "Put the product in a positive form"}, {"id": "the-first-two-moments-already-reveal-the-geometry", "title": "The first two product moments"}, {"id": "build-the-s-transform", "title": "Build the S-transform"}, {"id": "two-examples-that-fix-the-normalization", "title": "Checking the S-transform normalization"}, {"id": "recovering-a-law-from-its-s-transform", "title": "Recovering a law from its S-transform"}, {"id": "try-it-before-continuing", "title": "Exercises"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>Addition now has a workable recipe: identify the marginal laws, justify freeness, add their R-transforms, and recover the law of the sum.</p>\n<p>Products raise a preliminary problem. Even if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span> are positive semidefinite, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(AB\\)</span> need not be Hermitian. What is the positive matrix whose spectral distribution we should study?</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"put-the-product-in-a-positive-form\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"put-the-product-in-a-positive-form\">Put the product in a positive form</h2>\n<p>For positive semidefinite <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A,B\\)</span>, use <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nP=A^{1/2}BA^{1/2}.\n\\]</span> It is positive semidefinite because <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nx^*Px=(A^{1/2}x)^*B(A^{1/2}x)\\geq0.\n\\]</span> Cyclicity gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(P^k)=\\operatorname{Tr}((AB)^k).\n\\]</span> For invertible <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>, the matrices are similar: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nA^{-1/2}(AB)A^{1/2}=A^{1/2}BA^{1/2}.\n\\]</span> For singular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>, the determinant identity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\det(I-tXY)=\\det(I-tYX)\\)</span> still gives the same characteristic polynomial, with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=A^{1/2}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=A^{1/2}B\\)</span>. Nevertheless, the positive representative is preferable for spectral analysis and numerical eigensolvers.</p>\n<p>For bounded positive free variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a,b\\)</span> in a tracial operator-algebra setting, the law of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a^{1/2}ba^{1/2}\\)</span> is called <strong>multiplicative free convolution</strong>, written <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mu_a\\boxtimes\\mu_b.\n\\]</span> It is determined by the marginal laws and freeness. Our assumptions of positivity and boundedness make the law and square root unambiguous.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"the-first-two-moments-already-reveal-the-geometry\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"the-first-two-moments-already-reveal-the-geometry\">The first two product moments</h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=\\tau(a)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\tau(b)\\)</span>. Freeness gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\tau(ab)=\\alpha\\beta.\n\\]</span> Write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=\\alpha1+a_0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b=\\beta1+b_0\\)</span>, where the subscripted variables are centered. Expanding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(abab\\)</span>, terms with one centered factor vanish. Terms with two centered factors survive only when they use the same variable. The terms with three centered factors vanish by freeness and cyclicity, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau(a_0b_0a_0b_0)=0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Therefore <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tau(abab)\n&amp;=\\alpha^2\\beta^2+\\beta^2\\tau(a_0^2)+\\alpha^2\\tau(b_0^2)\\\\\n&amp;=\\beta^2m_2(a)+\\alpha^2m_2(b)-\\alpha^2\\beta^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span> For mean-one variables with variances <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_a,v_b\\)</span>, the product has mean one and variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_a+v_b\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Classically independent positive variables instead give <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[X^2Y^2]=\\mathbb E[X^2]\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\)</span>, hence variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_a+v_b+v_av_b\\)</span> when both means are one. The same two marginal spectra can produce a different second product moment depending on how their directions interact.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"build-the-s-transform\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"build-the-s-transform\">Build the S-transform</h2>\n<p>Define the moment series without its constant term: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\psi_\\mu(t)=\\int\\frac{tx}{1-tx}\\,d\\mu(x)\n=\\sum_{k\\geq1}m_kt^k.\n\\]</span> Assume <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_1\\neq0\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\psi_\\mu'(0)=m_1\\)</span>, it has a local compositional inverse <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\chi_\\mu\\)</span> near zero. Set <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{S_\\mu(w)=\\frac{1+w}{w}\\chi_\\mu(w).}\n\\]</span> The apparent singularity at zero is removable. Because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\chi(w)=w/m_1+O(w^2)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS_\\mu(0)=\\frac1{m_1}.\n\\]</span> For a positive law, the only zero-mean case is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta_0\\)</span>, whose product law is trivially <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta_0\\)</span>. A centered sign variable, however, does not have this ordinary S-transform: its moment series has no linear term. We cannot reuse the sign example without changing the setup.</p>\n<p>The multiplicative analogue of R-transform additivity is the theorem <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{S_{\\mu\\boxtimes\\nu}(w)=S_\\mu(w)S_\\nu(w).}\n\\]</span> This is a substantive theorem about free products, not a consequence of the inverse-function definition alone. Its combinatorial proof uses noncrossing partitions and their complements. See <a href=\"https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/speicher/publikationen/Nica-Speicher.pdf\">Nica and Speicher, Lectures 14 and 18</a>.</p>\n<p>A short expansion connects it to our earlier calculation. Inverting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\psi(t)=m_1t+m_2t^2+\\cdots\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS(w)=\\frac1{m_1}\n+\\left(\\frac1{m_1}-\\frac{m_2}{m_1^3}\\right)w+O(w^2).\n\\]</span> For mean-one laws this is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-vw+O(w^2)\\)</span>. Multiplying the two series adds their variances, exactly as the direct mixed-moment calculation predicted.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"two-examples-that-fix-the-normalization\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"two-examples-that-fix-the-normalization\">Checking the S-transform normalization</h2>\n<p>For a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\psi(t)=\\frac{ct}{1-ct},\\quad\n\\chi(w)=\\frac{w}{c(1+w)},\\quad\nS_{\\delta_c}(w)=\\frac1c.\n\\]</span> Thus multiplying by a constant rescales the law and divides its S-transform by that constant.</p>\n<p>For a projection <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=p^*=p^2\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau(p)=\\alpha&gt;0\\)</span>, every positive-order moment is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha\\)</span>. Its law is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((1-\\alpha)\\delta_0+\\alpha\\delta_1\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\psi_p(t)=\\frac{\\alpha t}{1-t},\\quad\n\\chi_p(w)=\\frac{w}{\\alpha+w},\\quad\n\\boxed{S_p(w)=\\frac{1+w}{\\alpha+w}.}\n\\]</span> If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> is a free projection with trace <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS_{pqp}(w)=\\frac{(1+w)^2}{(\\alpha+w)(\\beta+w)}.\n\\]</span> Although <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> are projections, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(pqp\\)</span> generally is not. Randomly oriented subspaces produce intermediate eigenvalues between zero and one.</p>\n<p>Its first two moments, using the calculation above, are <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nm_1=\\alpha\\beta,\\qquad\nm_2=\\alpha\\beta(\\alpha+\\beta-\\alpha\\beta).\n\\]</span> At <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=\\beta=1/2\\)</span>, these are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/4\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3/16\\)</span>. An intersection indicator for independent commuting scalar events would instead have both moments equal to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/4\\)</span>. This gives a concrete distinction between multiplying free projections and multiplying independent indicator variables.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"recovering-a-law-from-its-s-transform\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"recovering-a-law-from-its-s-transform\">Recovering a law from its S-transform</h2>\n<p>Use the same Cauchy-transform convention as Part III. Then <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\psi(1/z)=zG(z)-1.\n\\]</span> Writing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=zG(z)-1\\)</span>, the inverse relation is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\chi(w)=1/z\\)</span>. Substituting into the S-transform definition gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{zwS(w)=1+w,\\qquad G(z)=\\frac{1+w}{z}.}\n\\]</span> Choose the solution with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\to0\\)</span> as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\to\\infty\\)</span> and the correct upper-half-plane behavior for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>. This is the computational route we will use for covariance products in the final post.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-continuing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S_{\\delta_1}\\)</span>, and why is that necessary?</li>\n<li>Show directly that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S_{ca}(w)=S_a(w)/c\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>If two free positive mean-one variables each have second moment two, what is the second moment of their product?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>It is one, the multiplicative identity. Multiplying by the identity operator must preserve the law.</li>\n<li><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\psi_{ca}(t)=\\psi_a(ct)\\)</span>, so the compositional inverse is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\chi_a(w)/c\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Each variance is one. The product variance is two and its mean is one, so its second moment is three.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n\n</section>\n"}, {"number": 6, "title": "Covariance matrices and their products", "subtitle": "From Marchenko-Pastur to the moments 1, 3, 12, 55", "description": "Fix the sample-covariance normalization, derive its transforms, and predict the spectrum of a positive product.", "date": "2026-10-17", "toc": [{"id": "fix-the-aspect-ratio-before-naming-the-law", "title": "Aspect ratio and the Marchenko-Pastur law"}, {"id": "derive-the-density-and-remember-the-zero-atom", "title": "The density and the atom at zero"}, {"id": "find-the-s-transform-without-another-integral", "title": "Find the S-transform without another integral"}, {"id": "a-complete-product-calculation-in-the-square-case", "title": "Multiplying square covariance matrices"}, {"id": "check-the-product-numerically", "title": "Check the product numerically"}, {"id": "what-i-would-check-before-using-this-on-a-new-problem", "title": "Assumptions to check on a new problem"}, {"id": "try-it-before-finishing", "title": "Exercises"}, {"id": "further-reading", "title": "Further reading"}], "html": "\n\n\n<p>A sample covariance matrix is already a product. If the columns of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times m}\\)</span> are independent observations, then <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nC=\\frac1mXX^*\n\\]</span> measures their spread across directions. Even when the population covariance is the identity, the eigenvalues of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> need not all be close to one when the dimension and sample count grow together.</p>\n<p>We will calculate the limiting law, then multiply two independently oriented covariance matrices. This brings both transforms from the series into one example.</p>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"fix-the-aspect-ratio-before-naming-the-law\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"fix-the-aspect-ratio-before-naming-the-law\">Aspect ratio and the Marchenko-Pastur law</h2>\n<p>Take independent standard complex Gaussian entries, with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E X_{ij}=0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E|X_{ij}|^2=1\\)</span>. Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n,m\\to\\infty\\)</span> with <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\frac nm\\longrightarrow\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty).\n\\]</span> We normalize the spectral measure by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/n\\)</span>, over all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> eigenvalues of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>. Its limiting mean is one.</p>\n<p>The Marchenko-Pastur theorem identifies the limit. One moment formulation for this Gaussian model is <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nm_k=\\sum_{\\pi\\in NC(k)}\\gamma^{k-|\\pi|}.\n\\]</span> This result comes from Gaussian moment expansion and counting the index sums that survive at leading order. We use that random-matrix theorem as input, rather than proving it from the transform identities. <a href=\"https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/speicher/publikationen/Nica-Speicher.pdf\">Nica and Speicher, Lecture 22</a> develop Gaussian moment methods and their connection to Wishart matrices.</p>\n<p>Comparing with the free moment-cumulant formula gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\kappa_k=\\gamma^{k-1},\\qquad\nR(w)=\\frac1{1-\\gamma w}.\n\\]</span> In particular, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_1=1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_2=1+\\gamma\\)</span>, and the limiting spectral variance is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Different references use different parameters for the free Poisson or Marchenko-Pastur law. In the free-Poisson convention with rate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span> and jump size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span>, cumulants are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda c^k\\)</span>. Our convention has <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda=1/\\gamma\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=\\gamma\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"derive-the-density-and-remember-the-zero-atom\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"derive-the-density-and-remember-the-zero-atom\">The density and the atom at zero</h2>\n<p>Insert the R-transform into the Cauchy-transform equation: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nz=\\frac1G+\\frac1{1-\\gamma G}.\n\\]</span> After rearranging, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\gamma zG^2-(z+\\gamma-1)G+1=0.\n\\]</span> Let <span class=\"math display\">\\[\na=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\qquad b=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2.\n\\]</span> The branch satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G(z)\\sim1/z\\)</span> is <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nG(z)=\\frac{z+\\gamma-1-\\sqrt{(z-a)(z-b)}}{2\\gamma z},\n\\]</span> where the square root is asymptotic to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span> at infinity. Stieltjes inversion gives the law <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mu_\\gamma=\n\\left(1-\\frac1\\gamma\\right)_+\\delta_0\n+\\frac{\\sqrt{(b-x)(x-a)}}{2\\pi\\gamma x}\n\\mathbf1_{(a,b)}(x)\\,dx.\n}\n\\]</span> Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\((r)_+=\\max(r,0)\\)</span>. The continuous density has mass <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\min(1,1/\\gamma)\\)</span>, with the rest at zero.</p>\n<p>The atom is also visible before taking a limit. When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n&gt;m\\)</span>, a Gaussian <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\times m\\)</span> matrix has rank <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span> almost surely. Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> has exactly <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n-m\\)</span> zero eigenvalues, a fraction approaching <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/\\gamma\\)</span>. Keeping only the nonzero eigenvalues and renormalizing them would produce a different probability measure.</p>\n<div class=\"quarto-figure quarto-figure-center\">\n<figure class=\"figure\">\n<p><a aria-label=\"Open figure at full size in a new tab\" class=\"figure-link\" data-gallery=\"quarto-lightbox-gallery-1\" href=\"assets/marchenko-pastur.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Sample covariance spectra for aspect ratios one half, one, and two, with the predicted continuous densities. The final panel has an additional zero atom of mass one half.\"><img alt=\"Three covariance histograms with Marchenko-Pastur density curves for gamma 0.5, 1, and 2. The last panel labels zero mass 0.5. Its positive histogram has total area 0.5 rather than being renormalized to one.\" class=\"img-fluid figure-img\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/marchenko-pastur.svg\"/></a></p>\n\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p>Each panel uses one matrix with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n=320\\)</span>. In the final panel, the zero eigenvalues are omitted from the density bars, but their probability mass is retained in the normalization and annotated separately.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"find-the-s-transform-without-another-integral\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"find-the-s-transform-without-another-integral\">Find the S-transform without another integral</h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=\\psi(t)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M(t)=1+w\\)</span>. The moment-cumulant equation from Part III gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nw=t(1+w)R(t(1+w))\n=\\frac{t(1+w)}{1-\\gamma t(1+w)}.\n\\]</span> Solve for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)</span>: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\chi(w)=\\frac{w}{(1+w)(1+\\gamma w)}.\n\\]</span> Consequently, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{S_{\\mu_\\gamma}(w)=\\frac1{1+\\gamma w}.}\n\\]</span> For two free covariance laws with aspect ratios <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2\\)</span>, multiplication gives <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS(w)=\\frac1{(1+\\gamma_1w)(1+\\gamma_2w)}.\n\\]</span> The first derivative at zero confirms that the mean-one product has variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma_1+\\gamma_2\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"a-complete-product-calculation-in-the-square-case\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"a-complete-product-calculation-in-the-square-case\">Multiplying square covariance matrices</h2>\n<p>Set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma_1=\\gamma_2=1\\)</span>. Consider independent square Gaussian covariance matrices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_n,B_n\\)</span>, each normalized by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/n\\)</span>, and the positive product <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nP_n=A_n^{1/2}B_nA_n^{1/2}.\n\\]</span> These independent Gaussian covariance ensembles are unitarily invariant and asymptotically free. Their norm control and marginal convergence justify using the positive product law. The limiting S-transform is <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS(w)=\\frac1{(1+w)^2}.\n\\]</span> With <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t=\\chi(w)\\)</span>, this gives the exceptionally simple equation <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\boxed{w=t(1+w)^3.}\n\\]</span> Because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=\\sum_{k\\geq1}m_kt^k\\)</span>, its coefficients are the desired moments. The first terms follow by substitution: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nw=t+3t^2+12t^3+55t^4+\\cdots.\n\\]</span> For all orders, the Lagrange inversion identity for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=t\\phi(w)\\)</span> states <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n[t^k]w=\\frac1k[u^{k-1}]\\phi(u)^k.\n\\]</span> Using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\phi(u)=(1+u)^3\\)</span> yields <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nm_k=\\frac1k\\binom{3k}{k-1}\n=\\boxed{\\frac1{2k+1}\\binom{3k}{k}}.\n\\]</span> These are the order-two Fuss-Catalan numbers. The terminology is useful, but the equation <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=t(1+w)^3\\)</span> already gives a direct way to compute them.</p>\n<p>The same equation locates the positive right edge. With <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t=1/z\\)</span>, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nz=\\frac{(1+w)^3}{w}.\n\\]</span> Starting at large positive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span>, the physical solution has small positive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\)</span>. Its first positive turning point satisfies <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\frac{d}{dw}\\frac{(1+w)^3}{w}\n=\\frac{(1+w)^2(2w-1)}{w^2}=0.\n\\]</span> Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=1/2\\)</span> and the right endpoint is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=27/4\\)</span>. The resulting law has support <span class=\"math inline\">\\([0,27/4]\\)</span>. A detailed analysis of this branch and its density appears in <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/1407.1282#S4.SS2.SSS1\">Młotkowski, Nowak, Penson, and Życzkowski, Section IV.2.1</a>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"check-the-product-numerically\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"check-the-product-numerically\">Check the product numerically</h2>\n<p>For eight independent draws at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n=384\\)</span>, the first four moments are:</p>\n<div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<table class=\"caption-top table\">\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Moment</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Exact limit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Empirical mean</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Sample standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.00021</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00288</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3.00147</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.01510</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">12.00329</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.09935</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">55</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">54.96451</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.73908</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>The last column is the sample standard deviation across the eight matrices, not a confidence interval or an error bound. Higher moments fluctuate more because they give greater weight to large eigenvalues.</p>\n<div class=\"quarto-figure quarto-figure-center\">\n<figure class=\"figure\">\n<p><a aria-label=\"Open figure at full size in a new tab\" class=\"figure-link\" data-gallery=\"quarto-lightbox-gallery-2\" href=\"assets/covariance-product.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"A histogram of eigenvalues of eight positive covariance products, with the limiting right endpoint marked at 27/4.\"><img alt=\"A covariance-product spectral histogram concentrated near zero with a long right tail. A dashed line marks the limiting support endpoint 6.75.\" class=\"img-fluid figure-img\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/covariance-product.svg\"/></a></p>\n\n</figure>\n</div>\n<p>The histogram is an illustration. The moment table compares the numerical calculation with exact quantities derived before the experiment. Neither establishes a theorem about finite-dimensional extreme eigenvalues.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"resources/free_probability_figures.py\">full script</a> constructs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_n^{1/2}\\)</span> by a Hermitian eigendecomposition and diagonalizes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_n^{1/2}B_nA_n^{1/2}\\)</span>. It also checks the first eight Fuss-Catalan coefficients by recursively expanding the cubic equation, independently of the closed formula. <a data-chapter=\"4\" href=\"?page=article&amp;chapter=4\">Part IV</a> gives reproduction instructions.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"what-i-would-check-before-using-this-on-a-new-problem\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"what-i-would-check-before-using-this-on-a-new-problem\">Assumptions to check on a new problem</h2>\n<p>The calculations depend on more than knowing the marginal eigenvalue histograms. First specify the normalized matrix and the aspect ratio. Then justify the joint asymptotic-freeness statement. For a product, choose the positive representative and check that the mean permits the ordinary S-transform. Finally, retain the correct Cauchy-transform branch and any atoms when recovering the law.</p>\n<p>These checks prevent several tempting mistakes: multiplying eigenvalues coordinate by coordinate, discarding zero eigenvalues, or choosing a convenient root of an algebraic equation without checking its asymptotics.</p>\n<p>The series began with the observation that individual eigenvalues leave out relative geometry. Freeness supplies one precise limiting description of that geometry. Cumulants, the R-transform, and the S-transform then turn it into calculations we can test.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"try-it-before-finishing\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"try-it-before-finishing\">Exercises</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What are the limiting mean, variance, and zero mass for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma=2\\)</span>?</li>\n<li>Compute the second moment of a free product with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma_1=1/2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma_2=2\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>What moment equation would a product of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span> free square covariance variables satisfy?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-sketch\"><summary>Answer sketches</summary>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Mean one, variance two, zero mass one half. The mean is averaged over all eigenvalues, including the zeros.</li>\n<li>The variance is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/2+2=5/2\\)</span>, so the second moment is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1+5/2=7/2\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Its S-transform is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((1+w)^{-r}\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w=t(1+w)^{r+1}\\)</span>. Lagrange inversion gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m_k=\\binom{(r+1)k}{k}/(rk+1)\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"level2\" id=\"further-reading\">\n<h2 class=\"anchored\" data-anchor-id=\"further-reading\">Further reading</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/speicher/publikationen/Mingo-Speicher.pdf\">Mingo and Speicher, Free Probability and Random Matrices</a> develops the random-matrix foundations. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.3453\">Penson and Życzkowski</a> study the Fuss-Catalan laws arising from squared singular values of products. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.1282\">Młotkowski and collaborators</a> explain the S-transform calculations and explicit densities. Their normalization conventions should be checked against the mean-one convention used here.</p>\n\n</section>\n"}]
